    <p>Price: ₹2499</p>
    <button onclick="alert('Added to cart!')">Add to Cart</button>

    <h3>Bluetooth Speaker</h3>
    <p>Price: ₹999</p>
    <button onclick="alert('Added to cart!')">Add to Cart</button>
</body>
</html>



Save with Ctrl + S and close Notepad.

Now we have two web pages: index.html and products.html.
Step 5: Create the Dockerfile
To avoid the .txt extension problem we encountered with Notepad, create the file directly using PowerShell.

Run this complete command:
@'
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
COPY products.html /usr/share/nginx/html/products.html
EXPOSE 80
'@ | Set-Content -Path Dockerfile -Encoding ascii


The file will be created with the exact name Dockerfile, without .txt.

Check the files:
Get-ChildItem -Name


You should see:
Dockerfile
index.html
products.html


Remember: The Dockerfile contains instructions for building our website's Docker image.
Step 6: Build the Docker image
Run:
docker build -t shopeasy .


Wait for the command to finish successfully.

Verify the image:
docker images


You should see shopeasy:latest in the output.

Remember: docker build creates the image using the Dockerfile.
Step 7: Run the Docker container
Initially, we tried port 8080, but it was already in use. Therefore, we changed the host port to 8081.

First, remove the failed container if it still exists:
docker rm shopeasy-container


If PowerShell says the container does not exist, that's fine; continue.

Now run:
docker run -d --name shopeasy-container -p 8081:80 shopeasy


A long container ID should appear if the command succeeds.

Here, 8081 is the port on your laptop, and 80 is the port inside the container.
Step 8: Verify the running container
Run:
docker ps


You should see shopeasy-container with a port mapping similar to:
0.0.0.0:8081->80/tcp


This confirms that the container is running.
Step 9: Open the website
Open Google Chrome and visit these URLs.
Page 1: Home page

http://localhost:8081
Open Home Page

Page 2: Products page

http://localhost:8081/products.html
Open Products Page

Check that both pages load, the navigation links work, and clicking Add to Cart displays a message.

The cart is only a demonstration; it does not store products or process payments.
Step 10: Stop and restart the website
To stop the website:
docker stop shopeasy-container


To start it again:
docker start shopeasy-container


Then reopen http://localhost:8081.
Step 11: Practical journal — write this in your notebook
Procedure: Created two HTML pages, Home and Products, for an E-commerce website. Created a Dockerfile using the Nginx Alpine image. Built the Docker image using the docker build command and ran the container using the docker run command. Verified the running container using docker ps and accessed both web pages through a browser.

Result: Successfully deployed a two-page E-commerce website using Docker.

Conclusion: Docker allows us to package and run a web application inside a container.
Viva questions
Question	Simple answer
What is Docker?	Docker is a platform used to run applications inside containers.
What is a Dockerfile?	A file containing instructions to build a Docker image.
What is an image?	A package used to create a container.
What is a container?	A running instance of an image.
Why did we use Nginx?	To serve our HTML web pages through a browser.
Why did we use port 8081?	Because port 8080 was already occupied.
What is docker build?	It creates a Docker image.
What is docker run?	It creates and starts a container from an image.

